boundary-condition-magnetism-40fbf320.symmetry_origin:
This boundary condition is derived from Gauss's law for magnetism, which states that the divergence of the magnetic field is always zero (<inline-formula>
<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>∇</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math>

). Integrating this equation over an infinitesimal Gaussian pillbox that straddles the boundary leads to the continuity of the normal component of the magnetic field.

boundary-condition-magnetism-40fbf320.limits_and_boundary:
As the physical properties of the two media become identical, the boundary condition remains valid, simply stating that the normal component of the magnetic field is continuous across a homogeneous medium. If the interface were to contain surface currents (<inline-formula>
<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">K</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></math>

), the boundary condition would be modified to include a jump in the tangential component of the magnetic field, but the normal component would still be continuous.
